arm: build on M-profile, as a libffi that refuses to work

libffi has no Cortex-M port. src/arm/sysv.S is written in ARM mode, which
M-profile does not have at all:

  ../src/arm/sysv.S:104: Error: selected processor does not support ARM opcodes

and switching it to Thumb -- the file has a Thumb path already, used by Windows
on ARM -- gets further and then stops on things that are not a mode question:

  Error: selected processor does not support `stc p11,cr0,[sp],{16}' in Thumb mode
  Error: selected processor does not support `ldc p10,cr0,[r2]' in Thumb mode
  Error: cannot honor width suffix -- `ldr pc,1f'
  Error: invalid Hi register with immediate

The VFP save and restore are spelled as coprocessor loads and stores so they
assemble without -mfpu, and that form does not exist on M-profile in either
mode. The closure path builds its trampoline as machine code at run time.
Porting all of it is a real piece of work, and this is not it.

What this does is make libffi build. On M-profile the assembly compiles to
nothing and every C entry point returns FFI_BAD_ABI -- present, so the failure
is a return value where libffi is called rather than an undefined symbol at
link time in an unrelated package, and immediate, so nothing proceeds on a
half-prepared cif. ffi_call() cannot report a failure through its signature and
aborts instead; returning quietly would leave the caller reading an
uninitialised return value.

The reason for wanting that: glib needs libffi only for gobject's generic
closure marshaller. A program that links glib-2.0 and gmodule-2.0 without
touching GObject -- Midnight Commander, for one -- never calls any of these.
Without this, glib cannot be configured on the target and neither can anything
below it.

No other ARM target changes: __ARM_ARCH_ISA_ARM is defined wherever A32 exists.

Upstream status: not submitted. Upstream would want the port, not this.

--- a/src/arm/sysv.S
+++ b/src/arm/sysv.S
@@ -26,6 +26,28 @@
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
 
 #ifdef __arm__
+
+/* M-profile (Cortex-M) has no A32 instruction set, and libffi has no port to
+ * it: everything below is written in ARM mode, and the parts that are not --
+ * the VFP save and restore -- are spelled as coprocessor loads and stores
+ * (stc/ldc p10, p11), a form the assembler rejects on M-profile whichever mode
+ * it is in. The closure path additionally builds its trampoline as machine
+ * code at run time.
+ *
+ * Porting that is a real piece of work and is not attempted here. What this
+ * does instead is make the file empty on M-profile, so libffi builds and
+ * installs, and src/arm/ffi.c returns FFI_BAD_ABI from every entry point. A
+ * caller that actually uses libffi finds out immediately and by return value.
+ *
+ * The reason for wanting a libffi that builds but does not work: glib needs it
+ * only for gobject's generic closure marshaller, and a program that links
+ * glib-2.0 and gmodule-2.0 without touching GObject -- Midnight Commander, for
+ * one -- never reaches a single one of these functions. Without this, glib
+ * cannot be configured at all and neither can anything downstream of it.
+ */
+#ifndef __ARM_ARCH_ISA_ARM
+	/* Nothing. See above. */
+#else
 #define LIBFFI_ASM
 #include <fficonfig.h>
 #include <ffi.h>
@@ -449,6 +471,7 @@
 ARM_FUNC_END(ffi_arm_trampoline)
 
 #endif /* FFI_EXEC_TRAMPOLINE_TABLE */
+#endif /* __ARM_ARCH_ISA_ARM */
 #endif /* __arm__ */
 
 #if defined __ELF__ && defined __linux__
--- a/src/arm/ffi.c
+++ b/src/arm/ffi.c
@@ -37,6 +37,67 @@
 #include <tramp.h>
 #include "internal.h"
 
+#ifndef __ARM_ARCH_ISA_ARM
+/* M-profile: there is no port. See the note at the top of src/arm/sysv.S for
+ * what stands in the way and why a libffi that refuses to work is still worth
+ * building.
+ *
+ * Every entry point is present and returns FFI_BAD_ABI, so the failure arrives
+ * as a return value where libffi is used rather than as an undefined symbol at
+ * link time in some unrelated package. ffi_call() has no way to report a
+ * failure, so it aborts: returning silently would leave the caller reading an
+ * uninitialised return value, which is worse than stopping.
+ */
+
+ffi_status FFI_HIDDEN
+ffi_prep_cif_machdep (ffi_cif *cif)
+{
+  (void) cif;
+  return FFI_BAD_ABI;
+}
+
+ffi_status FFI_HIDDEN
+ffi_prep_cif_machdep_var (ffi_cif *cif, unsigned int nfixedargs,
+                          unsigned int ntotalargs)
+{
+  (void) cif; (void) nfixedargs; (void) ntotalargs;
+  return FFI_BAD_ABI;
+}
+
+void
+ffi_call (ffi_cif *cif, void (*fn) (void), void *rvalue, void **avalue)
+{
+  (void) cif; (void) fn; (void) rvalue; (void) avalue;
+  abort ();
+}
+
+void
+ffi_call_go (ffi_cif *cif, void (*fn) (void), void *rvalue, void **avalue,
+             void *closure)
+{
+  (void) cif; (void) fn; (void) rvalue; (void) avalue; (void) closure;
+  abort ();
+}
+
+ffi_status
+ffi_prep_closure_loc (ffi_closure *closure, ffi_cif *cif,
+                      void (*fun) (ffi_cif *, void *, void **, void *),
+                      void *user_data, void *codeloc)
+{
+  (void) closure; (void) cif; (void) fun; (void) user_data; (void) codeloc;
+  return FFI_BAD_ABI;
+}
+
+ffi_status
+ffi_prep_go_closure (ffi_go_closure *closure, ffi_cif *cif,
+                     void (*fun) (ffi_cif *, void *, void **, void *))
+{
+  (void) closure; (void) cif; (void) fun;
+  return FFI_BAD_ABI;
+}
+
+#else
+
 #if defined(_WIN32)
 #define WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN
 #include <windows.h>
@@ -918,4 +979,6 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+#endif /* __ARM_ARCH_ISA_ARM */
+
 #endif /* __arm__ or _M_ARM */
